#
# The golden vector for the PurRDF entailment-regime string boundary
# (`purrdf_validate::regime`). ONE artifact, consumed by every host: the Rust
# test in `purrdf-validate`, and later the C-ABI and WASM crates, all call
# `purrdf_validate::regime::check_regime_golden_vectors()` over these bytes, so a
# host that diverges fails against the same expectations instead of against a
# fixture that quietly stopped agreeing with the other two.
#
# Format (see `parse_regime_vectors`): a line starting with `@` is a directive;
# every other line belongs to the body the last body-directive opened; outside a
# body only blank lines and `#` comments are legal. A body line may therefore
# never begin with `@` — neither N-Quads nor a rendered report ever does.
#
#   @case <name>      opens a case
#   @regime <name>    the regime's CLI spelling
#   @input            body: the N-Quads/N-Triples input document
#   @program          body: the regime's own rule document; OPTIONAL, and absent
#                     is the empty program, which is what every regime but `rif`
#                     takes
#   @closure          body: the canonical N-Quads the closure must serialize to
#   @report           body: the report rendering the run must produce
#   @end              closes the case
#
# Every IRI is under `example.org`: PurRDF mints no vocabulary, and the only
# non-example.org IRIs below are the `rdf:`/`rdfs:`/`owl:` terms the entailment
# specifications themselves define.
#
# WHY THIS ARTIFACT MOVED: THE REPORT GRAMMAR IS AT 2, AND THE WITHHELD COUNT IS IN IT.
#
# No closure changed by one byte; every change below is in a `@report` body. Three
# things moved, and the banner went from 1 to 2 so they are visible rather than
# silent.
#
# `withheld-surrogates <n>` is NEW here. The shared renderer — the one Python, WASM
# and the C ABI all go through — never emitted it, while the CLI's own private
# renderer did. So `rdfD1`, `rdfD1a`, `rdfs14` and `rdfs14a` were invisible from
# every host but the command line, even though the count is the ONLY observable
# those four rules have: all four fire, every conclusion they reach mentions a
# minted blank node a SPARQL entailment regime may not answer with, so none of them
# can ever appear in a `fired` line. The rdfs case below now says 11 and the rdf case
# says 3, where a reader previously had a `boundary surrogate` paragraph saying the
# conclusions were "counted here" and no number anywhere.
#
# The trailing `overclaims false` line is GONE. It was a rendered constant: a report
# carries no completeness field to contradict its boundary list, because
# `ReasoningReport::completeness` derives the value from that list. A line that could
# only ever say `false` is a disclosure, not a gate, so the gate — and the
# `EntailError::Overclaim` it was supposed to raise, which no run could reach — was
# deleted rather than dressed up.
#
# An `inconsistency` witness now renders its GRAPH and its PREMISE TRIPLES
# (`inconsistency-graph`, `inconsistency-premise`). No case here shows them, because
# an inconsistent input has no closure and this artifact pairs an input with one;
# `purrdf_validate::regime::check_inconsistent_refusal` is the tri-host assertion
# that covers that path.
#
# AN EARLIER MOVE: THE BOUNDARY STOPPED REFUSING TWO REGIMES.
#
# No existing case changed by one byte. Two were ADDED, and the `@program`
# directive with them. `owl-direct` and `rif` used to be refused here by name,
# with a message listing the five spellings that were not — so this artifact,
# the one thing all four hosts check themselves against, covered five sevenths
# of the surface and the other two sevenths were pinned by an error string.
# `purrdf_entail::materialize` now takes a `Materialization` rather than a
# `Regime`, which carries each regime's own input, and this boundary passes
# them: `owl-direct` gets the empty basic graph pattern (a document boundary has
# no query, so what runs is the query-independent augmentation the tableau
# decides about the ontology's own named terms) and `rif` gets the rule set
# parsed from `@program`. Both now have a case, and the coverage check below
# ranges over all seven spellings.
#
# AN EARLIER MOVE: TWO `boundary` REASONS SAID THE OPPOSITE OF A THIRD.
#
# No closure changed and no case was added, removed or renamed. What changed is
# the prose of two `Construct::reason()` strings, which this artifact carries
# verbatim and therefore shipped to every host.
#
# `boundary triple-term` and `boundary datatype-value-space` both said, of
# rdfD1/rdfD1a and rdfs14/rdfs14a, "the evaluator mints no terms, so neither rule
# fires" — six occurrences across the cases below. That was true before the
# restricted chase landed and was never updated. `boundary surrogate`, in the
# same rendered report, says "All four FIRE", so a reader of one report was told
# both. It is the second sentence that is right: all four rules fire, the chase
# mints each surrogate as a frontier-addressed Skolem witness, and what is
# withheld is the SURROGATE TERM — a SPARQL entailment regime draws its answers
# from the scoping graph and a minted blank node is not in it. The two reasons
# now say that, and point at the surrogate boundary, which counts the withheld
# conclusions.

@case simple-copies-a-named-graph-verbatim
@regime simple
@input
<http://example.org/A> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/B> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/A> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> <http://example.org/g> .
@closure
<http://example.org/A> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/B> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> <http://example.org/g> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/A> .
@report
purrdf-reasoning-report 2
regime simple
completeness exact
budget join-steps 0
budget stored-facts 0
budget term-arena-bytes 0
contract-hash 4151090ce6c2ecdae843e351420ddcf10f79e525c60b4c6d07bebeabaa07fbd5
withheld-surrogates 0
inconsistency none
@end

@case rdf-types-every-predicate
@regime rdf
@input
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> .
@closure
<http://example.org/p> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> .
<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
@report
purrdf-reasoning-report 2
regime rdf
completeness exact-within-boundaries
fired rdfD2 2
boundary axiomatic-triples the FINITE part of the RDF and RDFS axiomatic triples — the fixed domain, range, type and sub-class statements about the RDF and RDFS vocabulary itself — is asserted as a premise, so every conclusion it licenses is drawn; two things are not. The container-membership family rdf:_1, rdf:_2, … is unbounded and no forward chase can materialize an infinite set, so rdfs12 fires only on a container property the graph itself types. And the asserted axioms are premises rather than conclusions, so the closure does not restate them: they are entailed but not emitted
boundary datatype-value-space rdfD1 and rdfD1a conclude about a FRESH blank node standing for a datatyped literal, or for an inhabited value space, and both FIRE: the restricted chase evaluates their existentially quantified heads. What is withheld is the SURROGATE itself — a SPARQL entailment regime draws its answers from the scoping graph, which no minted blank node is in — and the surrogate boundary is where those withheld conclusions are counted. rdfs1 recognizes the datatypes RDF 1.2 Semantics §8 makes mandatory (rdf:langString, rdf:dirLangString, xsd:string) and no others. The OWL 2 RL rules quantified over value spaces (dt-type2, dt-eq, dt-diff) DO fire, over the literals the dataset holds rather than over the infinitely many a value space contains, and value-space membership is decided through the candidate datatype's LEXICAL space, so a value in a datatype's value space whose lexical form is not in that datatype's lexical space is not found: "1.0"^^xsd:decimal is not typed xsd:integer. A datatype purrdf-xsd does not model is not judged either way
boundary surrogate rdfD1, rdfD1a, rdfs14 and rdfs14a all conclude about a FRESH blank node — the surrogate the specification writes `_:nnn`. All four FIRE: the restricted chase mints each surrogate as a frontier-addressed Skolem witness, so re-deriving the same obligation reuses the same witness and the fixpoint converges, and the closure is closed under everything the surrogate then licenses. What does not reach the answer is the surrogate ITSELF: every conclusion mentioning one is dropped at the materialization boundary and counted here. That is a requirement of SPARQL's entailment regimes, not a shortcut. The regime's answers are drawn from the SCOPING GRAPH, and a surrogate is not in it, so a solution binding a variable to one is not an answer the regime admits. The W3C case `rdfs13` is the proof: it asks `?L rdf:type rdfs:Literal` over a graph whose only literal is "foo" and requires ZERO rows, while rdfD1's surrogate gives `_:nnn rdf:type xsd:string` and hence `_:nnn rdf:type rdfs:Literal` through rdfs1, rdfs13 and rdfs9. Emitting that row would be WRONG, where withholding it is merely incomplete. Nothing surrogate-FREE is lost by the exclusion: replacing a term by a fresh blank node can only weaken a triple, so every conclusion that does not mention a surrogate was already licensed by the triple the surrogate stands for
budget join-steps 25
budget stored-facts 6
budget term-arena-bytes 433
contract-hash 9724540a02daf06f349ba8aecf52f9e3e21abf7042d1bfcc630172baa7f23ee3
withheld-surrogates 3
inconsistency none
@end

